## My billion-impression confession

For years, the bio on our team page bragged that my marketing strategies had helped businesses and nonprofits reach over one billion combined impressions.

I was proud of that number. I put it in proposals. I said it out loud at networking events, usually right before someone politely asked me what an impression was.

Here's the problem. In all my years doing this, not one founder has ever called me because they needed more impressions.

They called because payroll was coming. Because the slow season was coming. Because they had put their savings into a product, or a truck, or a storefront, and the phone wasn't ringing the way they'd hoped. A billion impressions never covered anyone's payroll.

I surf whenever five kids and a business let me, and here's the best way I can put it: counting impressions is like bragging about how many waves you watched from the parking lot. Great view. You're still dry. I spent a lot of years in that parking lot.

So I stopped caring about your reach. I care about your revenue. That idea, revenue over impressions, is the whole reason Revvia exists, and it's why I'm starting Founder's Notes right here.

## Why the agency world keeps selling you reach

Impressions are the easiest number in marketing to grow. Raise the budget and they go up. Post more and they go up. Boost a post to people who will never buy from you, and they go up again.

A number that only goes up is a beautiful thing to put in a monthly report. That's exactly why so many agencies lead with it.

If you've hired an agency before, you probably know the playbook:

- A 40-slide report full of reach, engagement, and click-through rate, with revenue somewhere near the back, if it shows up at all.
- A 12-month contract, so you can't leave when nothing moves.
- A senior person who sells you the account and a junior person who actually runs it.
- "Brand awareness" as the answer every time sales are flat.

I don't think most of the people in that system are villains. Plenty of them are good marketers stuck inside a broken model. But the model rewards activity you can screenshot, not results you can deposit.

And the founder pays for it. Privacy changes and crowded ad auctions have made every new customer more expensive to win than it was a few years ago. When each customer costs more, you can't afford to keep paying for attention that never turns into anything.

Here's the line I keep coming back to: **reach is a receipt for money spent. Revenue is a receipt for money made.**

## What we measure instead, and what it's shown us

We grade ourselves on numbers that show up in your bank account, not on your feed. These are the four we watch:

1. **Revenue, year over year, with spend held steady.** If revenue climbs only because the budget doubled, that's not growth. That's a bigger bill.
1. **New customers, and what each one cost to win.** Not clicks. Not leads. Customers.
1. **The lead-to-customer rate.** A form fill is a maybe. For EverSun Wellness, the goal was never form fills. It's paying members. So we connected their point-of-sale data back to Google and Meta, so the ad platforms learn from people who actually joined.
1. **What happens after the first sale.** Repeat purchases, retention, and lifetime value. That's where the profit lives.

Here's what that focus has produced for clients who've given us permission to share:

{% table %}
- Client
- What we measured
- What happened
---
- Peter Grimm Hats (heritage hat brand, Carlsbad)
- Year-over-year revenue at the same ad spend
- A peak summer month came in **41% above the same month last year**, with ad spend roughly flat
---
- Peter Grimm Hats
- Brand search campaign return
- About **30x** return, roughly double the 15x we'd call healthy for that kind of campaign
---
- Drain Mob (family-owned drain specialists, San Diego)
- Year-over-year revenue
- Up **33%** in an early-summer month, and it broke the seasonal dip: last year revenue slid going into summer; this year it grew
---
- Steady Builders (ADU and home builder, Carlsbad)
- Qualified leads tracked back to ads
- After 25 years of word of mouth only, advertising filled their proposal pipeline in the first two months
{% /table %}

I want to be honest about that 30x, because it's the kind of number I used to love too much. Brand search campaigns always return more, because the people searching your name already want you. That's my point. The 30x wasn't a paid media win. It was a brand win. The ad just collected what local creative, real festival moments, and years of craftsmanship had already earned.

Sometimes the right number makes us look worse. On one ecommerce account, the add-to-cart numbers looked fantastic. Then we dug in and found bots behind a big chunk of them. We cleaned it up, and the dashboard got uglier overnight. The account got smarter, because the ad platform stopped chasing shoppers who didn't exist.

I'll take an ugly dashboard that tells the truth over a pretty one that lies. Every time.

## How revenue-first changes the actual work

Caring about revenue isn't a slogan. It changes what we do on a Tuesday afternoon.

**We start at the cash register and work backward.** Before we scale a dollar of spend, we make sure we can see where customers actually come from. On a lot of accounts, the first month is unglamorous plumbing: fixing tracking, connecting sales data to the ad platforms, sending qualified leads back into Google so it learns what a real customer looks like. Nobody posts that on Instagram. It's still the most important work we do.

**We build the brand, because the brand makes the ads cheaper.** With Peter Grimm, we shot original creative with local San Diego models, put hats on real people at real festivals, and sharpened the voice before we ever turned up the budget. That's why the ads perform. Spend multiplies what a brand has already earned. It can't invent it.

**We make the creative ourselves.** Creative is now the biggest driver of results in paid social. So we produce it in-house instead of shipping it off to whoever's cheapest that month. When the people shooting the content also see the sales data, the next shoot gets better.

**We work month to month.** No 12-month lock-in. If we don't earn the next month, you leave. I'll admit that scared me when I first set it up. It turned out to be the best accountability system I've ever had.

**We tell you when it isn't marketing.** Sometimes the honest answer is that the offer, the pricing, or the follow-up is the real problem. A partner focused on revenue will tell you that, even when it isn't billable.

## Why this one is personal

I started Revvia while I was pastoring a church. I like to say I answer to a higher power than your checkbook, and I mean it. When a founder hands us their budget, I don't see a media line item. I see the person who took the risk, the crew who packs the orders, and the families depending on all of it.

I've got five kids at my dinner table. Not one of them has ever asked how many impressions Dad got this week. The older ones mostly want to know if I'm the reason ads interrupt their YouTube videos. (Sometimes. Sorry.) What they do understand is that the work has to actually provide. Your business is no different.

So here's something you can use today, whether you ever work with us or not.

**Five questions to ask your marketing partner this week:**

1. What did our marketing produce in revenue last month, and how do you know?
1. What does it cost us to win one new customer, and is that going up or down?
1. Which number in your report would you bet your own paycheck on?
1. If we cut the budget in half tomorrow, what would we actually lose?
1. What would make you tell us to stop paying you?

If your agency can't answer those, you don't have a reach problem. You have an accountability problem.

Impressions are where the work starts. Revenue is where it gets judged. If you want a partner who sees it that way, Revvia is a founder-led growth studio in Carlsbad, and I'd love to talk.

God Bless,

Tim Holt - Revvia Marketing

## Frequently asked questions

### What does "revenue over impressions" mean?

It means judging marketing by the revenue and customers it produces, not by how many people saw an ad. Impressions measure attention. Revenue measures whether that attention turned into a business result.

### What are vanity metrics in marketing?

Vanity metrics are numbers that look impressive but don't reliably connect to sales. Impressions, reach, followers, and likes are common examples. They're easy to grow with more spend and easy to report, which is why they show up in so many agency reports.

### Are impressions ever useful?

Yes, as an early signal. A new brand needs to be seen before it can be bought, and reach can show whether creative is getting in front of the right people. The problem is treating impressions as the report card instead of the starting line.

### What metrics should a founder track instead?

Start with four: revenue compared year over year at similar spend, the cost to win one new customer, the rate at which leads become paying customers, and repeat purchase or retention. Together they show whether marketing is building a business, not just a following.

### How does Revvia measure results for clients?

Revvia connects sales and customer data back to the ad platforms, so campaigns are optimized toward real customers instead of clicks. Results are reported as revenue, customer, and cost-per-customer changes over time, and clients work with Revvia month to month with no long-term contract.

